course: dictionary
lesson: 559
title: Who is the audience?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An audience is the group of people watching or listening to a show, film, talk or game. It can also mean the people a book, programme or advert is meant for. «Live shows often go wrong a little; audiences tend to remember the fun more than the mistakes.» Here audiences means the people watching the show.
explain: Writers think about their audience: who will read the text and what they need. «Every piece of writing has an audience (who reads it) and a purpose (why it is written), and these change the words you choose.» So a story for little children uses different words from a report for teachers.
explain: Audience is a noun, so it has a plural: audiences. You will meet phrases like in the audience, the audience clapped, live audience and target audience. A target audience is the group an advert is made for.
explain: Context helps you here too. «If you lose your place, staying calm and carrying on usually works, and school audiences are friendly.» Friendly audiences are kind, so if you make a mistake you can keep going.
fact: An audience is the group of people watching or listening.
fact: The audience can also be the people a book, programme or advert is meant for.
fact: A target audience is the group an advert is made for.
fact: «Cheating out» means standing a little open to the audience so they can see your face.
fact: Long classical pieces have several parts called movements.
exercise: «Live shows often go wrong a little; audiences tend to remember the fun more than the mistakes.» In this sentence, who are the audiences? | The actors who write the show | The lights and sound team | The teachers who mark the show | *The people watching or listening to the show
exercise: Ella writes an advert for a new toy. Who is her target audience? | Nobody at all | *Young children who might want the toy | Her teachers | Only the shop owner
exercise: «The audience clapped when the play ended.» Find and copy the word that means the people watching. | play | ended | clapped | *audience
test: Why does a writer think about their audience? | To make the text longer | So nobody can read it | To use as many hard words as possible | *To choose the right words for the people who will read it
test: «Cheating out» means standing a little open to the audience so they can see your face. What does the audience need to see? | Your shoes | The back of the stage | *Your face | The ceiling
exam: Mia is making a poster to tell parents about the school fair. Who is her audience? | Nobody, posters have no audience | The fair's rides | *The parents | Her pet hamster
